---

<NewLayout title="About Niki Tisza" description="UX & UI Designer | YouTuber | Sketchnote artist | Writer | Educator">
  <header class="page-hero split">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Welcome! <em>Thanks for stopping by.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">I assume you landed here to find out a little more about the person behind those words you just read. Am I right?! Well, here it goes.</p>
      <ul class="chips" aria-label="What I do">
        <li>I write.</li>
        <li>I design.</li>
        <li>I sketchnote.</li>
        <li><a href={YOUTUBE} target="_blank" rel="noopener">I create YouTube videos.</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="photo square">
      <img src="/images/pages/68bcafe8b84fe73564cda39a_niki.png" width="600" height="600" alt="Niki smiling, holding her glasses, in front of a brick wall" />
      <span class="note">I'm Niki.</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="section">
      <h2>Free <em>stuff</em></h2>
      <div class="tiles">
        {free.map((item) => (
          <a class="tile" href={item.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <span class="num">{item.n}.</span>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <span class="go">Get it on Gumroad →</span>
          </a>
        ))}
        <a class="tile" href={`http://linktr.ee/nikitisza${SRC}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          <span class="num">+</span>
          <h3>Everything else</h3>
          <p>Get them now → linktr.ee/nikitisza</p>
          <span class="go">Open Linktree →</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Most popular <em>articles</em></h2>
      <ul>
        {popular.map((post) => <li><a href={post.href} {...(post.href.startsWith('http') ? { target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener' } : {})}>{post.title}</a></li>)}
      </ul>

      <p>
        You can often find me creating videos about product design over on <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@nikitisza" target="_blank" rel="noopener">YouTube</a>, or sharing my thoughts on <a href="https://medium.com/@nikitisza" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Medium</a>. I'm passionate about mentoring the next generation of designers and giving back to the design community.
      </p>
      <p>
        My other passion is personal finance. As a former (burnt-out) investment banker, I became obsessed with understanding money and building healthy financial habits. The knowledge I've gained over decades shouldn't stay locked away, so I share practical money management advice through my <a href="https://www.instagram.com/itsmenikitisza" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Instagram</a> and <a href="https://www.tiktok.com/@niki.tisza" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TikTok</a> accounts, helping others navigate their money journey without the trial and error I went through.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Newsletter:</strong> I write a newsletter on product design, design career, self-development, writing, and how to be a part-time creator online. If that's your thing, <a href="https://nikitisza.substack.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">subscribe here</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Follow me on <a href={`https://www.linkedin.com/in/nikitisza/${SRC}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a> or <a href={`https://twitter.com/nikitisza${SRC}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Twitter</a>.
      </p>

      <h2><em>Storytime</em></h2>
      <p class="lead">All I ever wanted was to write.</p>
      <p>I've been fascinated with letters, words, sentences, and stories since I remember.</p>
      <p>I learnt the alphabet before going to elementary school.</p>
      <p>Soon, I moved on to writing my journals, probably as an escape from everything. It was my own little world where nothing bad could happen.</p>
      <p>Then I dipped my toes into fiction writing. I have 7 (unpublished) books in Hungarian.</p>
      <p>Then I moved on to blog writing. It was a little bit of fiction, a little bit of poetry. It was a colourful version, a somewhat fictive version of my life. I even received an award for it! I couldn't have been happier.</p>
      <p class="lead">When life happened...</p>
      <p>I <strong>stopped writing online</strong> end of 2010 to focus on learning English instead. But I kept up with journaling, though.</p>
      <p>A few years later, I posted an article (in English) on my first burnout.</p>
      <p>Despite my surprise, <strong>the article resonated with thousands of people</strong>.</p>
      <p>Of course, when so many read your writing, trolls came with it too.</p>
      <p>I got a couple of discouraging comments on the level of English I had at the time.</p>
      <p class="lead">It made me stop writing.</p>
      <p>I was a confident writer in Hungarian but not in English. After constant English learning and practicing for five years, I finally got back on track.</p>
      <p class="lead">Now, my goal is to write.</p>
      <p>I'm more confident in my ability to express ideas and thoughts in English. Way more confident than I was a decade ago.</p>
      <p>Of course, I still make mistakes. I'm not a native English speaker, after all.</p>
      <p>But I try my best to show up and create a sustainable writing habit besides a full-time job, and that's what matters.</p>
      <p>My full story about writing can be found <a href="/writing/all-i-ever-wanted-was-to-write">here.</a></p>

      <h2>My professional <em>experience</em></h2>
      <p>I have 10 years of experience making thousands of users' digital experience more enjoyable. My greatest strengths are making sense of complexity with analytical approach &amp; designing intuitive interfaces that encompass user needs, business goals, and technical feasibility.</p>
      <p>With an adventurous spirit, I honed my skills across a diverse set of industries and digital products, including an award winning mobile website builder, a revolutionary hospital service design, and a modern cloud-based e-commerce &amp; analytics tool.</p>
      <p>Recent New Zealand Government projects focused on climate change and simplifying the oddly complex pet export service. I also led two UX projects for the new Landonline product at Toitū Te Whenua Land Information New Zealand and Stuff as a UX architect.</p>
      <p>I'm currently based in Canada, working full-time at <a href="https://benevity.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Benevity</a>. I'm also a writer for <a href="https://logrocket.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LogRocket</a>.</p>
      <p>I'm always up for opportunities to consult, write, and collaborate creatively or simply share thoughts over a cup of coffee. I would love to hear from you.</p>
      <p>Check out the <a href="/services">services</a> I provide if you're looking for help with your project.</p>
      <p>I'm available for projects, collaborations, podcasts, talks, consultation, and leading workshops. If you're interested in partnership, take a look at my <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1MMXlGegT_G_3Wb6PsOU-l5JaRusp8CjUWAXCdyO6SqA/edit?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mediakit</a>.</p>

      <div class="pullquote">
        <span class="kicker">Byline for publications</span>
        <p>UX Practitioner &amp; Product Leader with over a decade of experience blending her product, design, engineering, and psychology knowledge. Specializes in product strategy &amp; discovery. Global portfolio includes PwC, Oracle, startups, and governmental projects. Published writer and YouTuber.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</NewLayout>
